\title{Period Divisibility under Dynamically Consistent Observation}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 25, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study pointwise recurrence through an observation map between discrete dynamical systems. Let $f:X\to X$ and $g:Y\to Y$ be arbitrary self-maps and let $h:X\to Y$ satisfy $h\circ f=g\circ h$. We prove that the minimal period of every observed state divides the minimal period of its source state. Thus a dynamically consistent observation may collapse a cycle only to one whose length is an arithmetic divisor of the original length. No topology, metric, compactness, continuity, cardinality condition, or finite-fiber hypothesis is needed. If $h$ is injective, every periodicity equation is reflected, so minimal periods and exact-period strata are preserved pointwise. For a source state of prime minimal period $p$, divisor rigidity gives a sharp dichotomy: its observation has minimal period $p$ or $1$. We give elementary proofs, finite-cycle realizations showing sharpness, algorithms for auditing recurrence, and applications to coarse-grained models, sensor design, symbolic dynamics, and cognitive observation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A discrete dynamical system repeatedly updates a state by a fixed rule. In applications, however, the full state is rarely visible. Measurement, categorization, feature extraction, and coarse-graining replace a hidden state with a reduced description. The observation can identify distinct phases of an orbit and make recurrence appear faster than it is.

The exact restriction on this apparent acceleration is arithmetic. When observation respects the dynamics, the observed minimal period divides the hidden minimal period. Information loss can shorten a rhythm, but it cannot produce an incompatible rhythm.

Given self-maps $f:X\to X$ and $g:Y\to Y$, an observation $h:X\to Y$ is dynamically consistent when
\[
h(f(x))=g(h(x))
\]
for every $x\in X$. This ensures that the observed variable evolves autonomously according to $g$. We establish period divisibility, exact preservation under injectivity, and a prime-period dichotomy. All conclusions are algebraic: neither topology nor finiteness is required.

\section{Preliminaries}

\begin{definition}[Discrete dynamics and iteration]
A discrete dynamical system on a set $X$ is a self-map $f:X\to X$. Its iterates are defined by $f^0=\id_X$ and $f^{k+1}=f\circ f^k$. They satisfy $f^{a+b}=f^a\circ f^b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Minimal and exact period]
A state $x\in X$ is periodic if $f^n(x)=x$ for some positive integer $n$. Its minimal period, written $\per_f(x)$, is the least such positive $n$. The state has exact period $n$ if $\per_f(x)=n$.
\end{definition}

A point of exact period $1$ is fixed. If $x$ has exact period $n$, then $x,f(x),\ldots,f^{n-1}(x)$ are pairwise distinct and are cyclically permuted by $f$.

\begin{lemma}[Return-time divisibility]\label{lem:return}
If $x$ has minimal period $d$ under $f$ and $f^n(x)=x$ for a positive integer $n$, then $d\mid n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $n=qd+r$ by Euclidean division, where $0\le r<d$. Since $f^d(x)=x$, iteration gives $f^{qd}(x)=x$. Hence
\[
x=f^n(x)=f^{qd+r}(x)=f^r(f^{qd}(x))=f^r(x).
\]
If $r>0$, this contradicts the minimality of $d$. Thus $r=0$, and $d\mid n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Dynamically consistent observation]
For systems $(X,f)$ and $(Y,g)$, an observation $h:X\to Y$ is dynamically consistent, or a semiconjugacy, when
\[
h\circ f=g\circ h.
\]
It is faithful when it is injective.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Iteration of consistency]\label{lem:iterate}
If $h\circ f=g\circ h$, then for every nonnegative integer $k$ and every $x\in X$,
\[
h(f^k(x))=g^k(h(x)).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $k$. The case $k=0$ is immediate. If the formula holds at $k$, then
\[
h(f^{k+1}(x))=h(f(f^k(x)))=g(h(f^k(x)))=g(g^k(h(x)))=g^{k+1}(h(x)).
\]
\end{proof}

\section{Period divisibility under observation}

\begin{theorem}[Observation Divisibility Theorem]\label{thm:divisibility}
Let $f:X\to X$ and $g:Y\to Y$ be self-maps, and let $h:X\to Y$ satisfy $h\circ f=g\circ h$. If $x\in X$ is periodic, then
\[
\per_g(h(x))\mid \per_f(x).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $n=\per_f(x)$. Then $f^n(x)=x$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:iterate} gives
\[
g^n(h(x))=h(f^n(x))=h(x).
\]
Thus $n$ is a return time of $h(x)$. If $d=\per_g(h(x))$, Lemma~\ref{lem:return} gives $d\mid n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No incompatible observed periods]
A state of exact period $n$ cannot be observed with exact period $d$ unless $d$ is a positive divisor of $n$.
\end{corollary}

Thus an exact period-$12$ state may be observed with period $1$, $2$, $3$, $4$, $6$, or $12$, but not period $5$, $7$, or $8$.

\begin{corollary}[Fixed points remain fixed]
If $f(x)=x$, then $g(h(x))=h(x)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:divisibility} is valid for arbitrary sets and functions. Compactness, metrizability, continuity, finite fibers, and measurability play no role. Observing period $d$ implies only that the hidden period is a multiple of $d$; equality requires faithfulness.
\end{remark}

\section{Faithful observation}

\begin{lemma}[Reflection of periodicity equations]\label{lem:reflect}
Assume $h\circ f=g\circ h$ and that $h$ is injective. If $g^n(h(x))=h(x)$, then $f^n(x)=x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:iterate} yields
\[
h(f^n(x))=g^n(h(x))=h(x).
\]
Injectivity gives $f^n(x)=x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Faithful Observation Theorem]\label{thm:faithful}
Let $h:X\to Y$ be injective and satisfy $h\circ f=g\circ h$. For every periodic state $x\in X$,
\[
\per_g(h(x))=\per_f(x).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $n=\per_f(x)$ and $d=\per_g(h(x))$. Theorem~\ref{thm:divisibility} gives $d\mid n$. Since $d$ is an observed return time, Lemma~\ref{lem:reflect} makes it a source return time, so minimality gives $n\mid d$. Hence $n=d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Transport of exact-period strata]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}, for every positive integer $n$ and every $x\in X$,
\[
\per_f(x)=n\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\per_g(h(x))=n.
\]
\end{corollary}

Define the exact-period spectrum by
\[
\Sigma(f)=\{n\in\mathbb N_{>0}:\text{there exists }x\in X\text{ with }\per_f(x)=n\}.
\]

\begin{corollary}[Spectrum equality under bijective semiconjugacy]
If $h$ is bijective and $h\circ f=g\circ h$, then $\Sigma(f)=\Sigma(g)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Injectivity transports the exact period of each $x$ to $h(x)$. Surjectivity ensures that every state of $Y$ is obtained in this way.
\end{proof}

\section{Prime-period rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-Period Observation Dichotomy]\label{thm:prime}
Suppose $h\circ f=g\circ h$. If $x$ has exact period $p$ under $f$, where $p$ is prime, then
\[
\per_g(h(x))=1\qquad\text{or}\qquad\per_g(h(x))=p.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:divisibility} says that the observed minimal period divides $p$. The only positive divisors of a prime are $1$ and $p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Faithful preservation of prime periods]
Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:prime}, if $h$ is injective, then $\per_g(h(x))=p$.
\end{corollary}

There is also a partition interpretation. On the prime cycle
\[
C=\{x,f(x),\ldots,f^{p-1}(x)\},
\]
the observation identifies phases with equal images. Consistency makes this equivalence invariant under cyclic rotation. A rotation-invariant equivalence on a prime cycle is either discrete or universal: all phases remain distinct, or all collapse to one fixed observation.

\section{Sharp finite-cycle realizations}

The divisor conclusion cannot be strengthened. Let $d\mid n$ and define
\[
X=\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z,\qquad Y=\mathbb Z/d\mathbb Z.
\]
Set
\[
f([k]_n)=[k+1]_n,\qquad g([j]_d)=[j+1]_d,
\]
and define $h([k]_n)=[k]_d$. The map is well-defined because $d\mid n$, and
\[
h(f([k]_n))=[k+1]_d=g(h([k]_n)).
\]
Every source state has exact period $n$, while every observed state has exact period $d$.

\begin{proposition}[Sharp realization of every divisor]
For every pair of positive integers $d,n$ with $d\mid n$, there are finite dynamical systems and a surjective dynamically consistent observation carrying an exact period-$n$ state to an exact period-$d$ state.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Use the residue-cycle construction above. Translation by one on $\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z$ has exact period $m$ at every point, and reduction modulo $d$ commutes with translation.
\end{proof}

For $n=6$, the construction realizes periods $1$, $2$, $3$, and $6$. Each observed phase has $n/d$ source phases above it. This uniform finite fiber is illustrative but is not required by Theorem~\ref{thm:divisibility}.

\section{Algorithms}

For finite systems, store $f$, $g$, and $h$ as arrays of successor and image indices.

\subsection{Consistency audit}
For each source state $x$, compare $h(f(x))$ with $g(h(x))$. Any failure proves that the observed variable is not a closed deterministic factor under the proposed update. The audit performs one comparison per state, requiring $O(|X|)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space beyond the arrays.

\subsection{Minimal-period detection}
Starting from $x$, repeatedly apply $f$ until returning to $x$. In a finite system, record first-visit times to detect either a return or entry into a cycle not containing $x$. This costs $O(|X|)$ time and $O(|X|)$ space. Floyd's cycle method can reduce auxiliary storage to $O(1)$.

\subsection{Period-transport audit}
After checking consistency, compute $n=\per_f(x)$ and $d=\per_g(h(x))$, then test $n\bmod d=0$. If $h$ is injective on the orbit, test $n=d$. Repeating direct orbit tracing for every initial state is at most quadratic, while cycle decomposition computes all periods in linear time.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\subsection{Coarse-grained models}
A compatible partition groups detailed states into macrostates that evolve deterministically. Its quotient map is a semiconjugacy, so apparent recurrence may accelerate only by a factor dividing the true period. If a proposed model sends an exact $n$-cycle to an exact $d$-cycle with $d\nmid n$, then consistency, period identification, or deterministic closure has failed.

\subsection{Cognitive and behavioral observation}
A full cognitive state may include latent memory, context, activation, and environmental variables, while an experiment records a response category. If categories evolve autonomously, a repeated response can conceal a longer latent cycle, but its period divides the latent period. A fixed observed response does not imply a fixed hidden state.

\subsection{Sensor design}
The Faithful Observation Theorem gives a design principle: separate every phase of each orbit whose exact recurrence matters. Global injectivity is stronger than necessary; injectivity on the target orbit suffices. Prime cycles are useful calibration objects because a nonconstant consistent observation of a prime cycle must retain the full prime period.

\subsection{Symbolic dynamics and logs}
A code from detailed states to labels defines a factor only when the next label depends solely on the current label and commutes with the update. If equal labels can have different successor labels, no single-valued observed update exists; a nondeterministic or history-dependent model is required.

\subsection{Topology versus probability}
These results concern pointwise recurrence. They assign no probability to periodic states and do not infer incidence from topological density. A dense set can have measure zero, while a nondense basin can have positive measure. Population statements require a probability measure and a specified event.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The divisibility law isolates the algebraic core of recurrence transport. A first extension is spectrum-level transport: under a surjective semiconjugacy, the observed spectrum lies in the divisor closure of the source spectrum; under bijective semiconjugacy the spectra are equal.

Global invariants demand additional structure. Topological entropy concerns exponential orbit complexity and requires topology and continuity. Li--Yorke scrambling depends on metric notions of proximity and separation, so two-way transport requires quantitative control such as uniform equivalence.

Quantitative observability is another direction. An interval map with an exact three-cycle can possess rich symbolic behavior, but an observation can erase it. A positive separation margin among observed orbit phases, combined with continuity control, may force exponential growth in distinguishable finite observation words.

In parametrized systems, robust recurrence should be described through attraction and basin measure rather than the density of periodic points. Tropical dynamics supplies a further analogue: an eigenvalue records additive drift, ordinary periodicity requires zero drift, and projectivization removes global additive offsets. A nonperiodic full motion may thereby become fixed after quotienting.

\section{Conclusion}

If a source state has minimal period $n$, every dynamically consistent observation has minimal period dividing $n$. Injectivity reflects all return equations and forces equality. For prime $n=p$, observation either preserves the $p$-cycle or collapses it to a fixed point. Residue cycles realize every allowed divisor, so the result is sharp.

The theory provides a baseline for analyzing recurrence under measurement. One first asks whether observation commutes with evolution and whether it separates the relevant states. Those structural facts determine the exact fate of the rhythm: faithful observation keeps the beat, while information loss can shorten it only by divisibility.

